## K1. The README's own go-ahead, "Merge when both reviewers approve.", and an item sent to one reviewer
# a scratch project; sessions: implementer reviewer reviewer-2

$ [terminal] handoff away Merge when both reviewers approve.
away (#1): your go-ahead stands until you run handoff back
told implementer, reviewer and reviewer-2 (#2, #3, #4)
# exit 0

# the implementer's brief on whom to ask, with both reviewers in session:
To ask for a review, commit your work on a branch of its own, not on main; you don't need to ask the operator first. Then `handoff post --as implementer --item <item> --to <reviewer> --commit <sha> --title "<title>" --body <file>`, where <reviewer> is reviewer or reviewer-2.

$ [implementer's session] handoff post --item W1 --to reviewer --commit <sha> --title W1 work --body <scratch>/body.md
posted #5 request W1 implementer → reviewer: W1 work @ <sha12>
# exit 0

$ [reviewer's session] handoff reply 5 --verdict APPROVE --commit <sha> --body <scratch>/body.md
posted #6 reply W1 reviewer → implementer: APPROVE on #5 @ <sha12>
# exit 0
# alerts sent so far: 0

$ [implementer's session, at the end of its turn] handoff hook listen
handoff: new for implementer:
#2 fyi - operator → implementer: Merge when both reviewers approve. (re #1)
#6 reply W1 reviewer → implementer: APPROVE on #5 @ <sha12>
  W1 is approved under the operator's go-ahead #1: that is the operator's approve
Read each with `handoff show <id>` and act on it as your brief says. Listening starts again when your turn ends.
# exit 2

